  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first: x swings grow once the learning rate is above 2/3 (for example 1). The ball starts at (0.5, 1.7).",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian with sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows start at the ball and are drawn 3× the true step length. Orange = the mini-batch step the ball will take; grey = −learning rate × the true slope. The step length readout gives the true length.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, the run number and the step number. Reset replays run 1 exactly. New run, keep last path uses the next noise values.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 8 (diverged). Above that the ball and trail are capped at the bowl's top edge, but the loss readout shows the true value.",
    "While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed. Changing a setting mid-run affects the next steps."
  ]
}